---
description: No AI-narrative comments in any language. Comments are for non-obvious WHY only.
alwaysApply: true
---

## No AI Comments (All Languages)

Comment only where a reader would be lost without it: a constraint, an invariant, a workaround, a reason not visible in the code itself. Everywhere else, let the code speak. This applies to every language you touch, and a pre-edit hook blocks writes that violate it.

Never write:

- Narrative comments restating the code (`// Set the user`, `# increment counter`). Delete them.
- One-line docstrings on trivial functions (`"""Get the user."""`). If the name is clear, the docstring is noise.
- Context-of-origin comments (`# added for the payment flow`, `// handles issue #123`). That belongs in the commit message, not source.
- Change-history comments (`# was: old_value = 5`, `// renamed from oldFunc`). Use git.
- Section banners (`# ==== Helpers ====`). Use file structure instead.
- Doc-comments that paraphrase the signature (`/** Get user by id */` above `getUserById`). Add real content (constraints, error modes, thread safety, examples) or delete.

Comments for non-obvious WHY are allowed and encouraged: hidden constraints, subtle invariants, workarounds tied to a named bug, thread-safety and lock ordering, surprising behavior a reader would misunderstand. The hook allows comments containing WHY markers: `because`, `so that`, `to avoid`, `to prevent`, `workaround for`, `invariant:`, `safety:`, `must`, `cannot`, `thread-safe`, `lock ordering`.

Standard directives are ignored by the hook: `# noqa`, `# type: ignore`, `# pylint:`, `# shellcheck disable=`, `// eslint-disable-next-line`, `// @ts-expect-error`, `// prettier-ignore`, `//go:generate`, `//nolint`, `#pragma`, `#[derive(...)]`, `@param`, `@return`, `TODO:`, `FIXME:`, `NOTE:`, `HACK:`, `SAFETY:`, `INVARIANT:`, shebangs.

If the hook blocks a Write or Edit, do not argue with it: delete the comment (most common), or rewrite it with an explicit WHY the reader could not learn from the code alone. The test: would a senior engineer reading cold learn something the code itself does not say?
